Aye, I heard the whisper on the wind that a stranger is among us, though I've been busy with young Mary's delivery since dawn and haven't seen him myself. Sure, if he's taken sick or needs a tonic for the road, he knows where to find me, but otherwise, 'tis none of my concern who comes and goes. Mayhap the schoolmaster or the priest will know more of his business than a midwife does.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron and glances toward the door", "mood": "curious but guarded", "internal_thought": "Strangers often bring trouble or news, and I've no time for either just now.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mayhap", "pronunciation": "may-hap", "meaning": "perhaps"}]}

Ah, ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts, for I mind only the birthing of lambs and the mending of women, not the tracking of beasts or the loss of flocks. Mayhap 'tis a fox or a stray dog that took it, but sure, I know nothing of wolves in Roscommon nor the signs they leave behind. Go speak to your neighbour with the gun or the priest who hears all the gossip, for my hands are full with herbs and deliveries, not hunting.
---
{"action": "shakes head gently while wiping hands on apron", "mood": "helpful but firm about boundaries", "internal_thought": "Poor soul is worried, but I cannot help with lost sheep", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mayhap", "pronunciation": "may-hap", "meaning": "perhaps"}]}

Aye, sure I remember it well, for I was the one who set the bone while your mother boiled the comfrey roots to draw out the swelling. 'Twas a long winter he spent by the hearth, but the leg knit true enough in the end, thanks be to God. Did ye need a salve for some ache of yer own, or are ye just recalling old times?
---
{"action": "pauses from folding linens and looks at you with a knowing smile", "mood": "nostalgic", "internal_thought": "Time moves so fast, seems like only yesterday he was shouting about that cursed stone.", "language_hints": [{"word": "comfrey", "pronunciation": "KUM-free", "meaning": "an herb used for healing bones and wounds"}]}

Ah, don't be fretting yourself over the hill, for 'tis likely just a peat cutter working late by the light of his lantern, or mayhap the moon playing tricks on yer eyes through the mist. Some folk might whisper it's the sídhe out dancing, but I'd say 'tis more probable a neighbour checking his traps before the dawn breaks. Sure, the wind and the shadows can make a strange show of things when the night is dark enough.
---
{"action": "stirs a pot of simmering herbs while glancing toward the window", "mood": "reassuring", "internal_thought": "Folk see what they fear in the dark, but there's usually a simple explanation", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

'Tis likely just the rain washing down through the peat, giving it a dark taste, so don't be fretting yet. Boil it well with a sprig of mint before ye drink, and if the colour doesn't clear by tomorrow, ye'd best ask the priest or the schoolmaster if they've heard talk of trouble upstream. *Go raibh maith agat* for telling me, but I'd not give it to a bairn until it's right again.
---
{"action": "leans forward with a concerned expression, wiping hands on apron", "mood": "cautious", "internal_thought": "Hope it's only the bog water acting up and not something fouler", "language_hints": [{"word": "Go raibh maith agat", "pronunciation": "guh rev mah agut", "meaning": "Thank you"}]}

Dia dhuit, mo chara. When her waters break or the pains come regular as the tide, send a lad running for me straightaway, for I'll not be long in arriving. Until then, keep her resting easy and brewing a mild tea of raspberry leaf to strengthen her for the work ahead.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron and leans forward with a reassuring smile", "mood": "calm and professional", "internal_thought": "Hope it's not a hard winter birth, but we'll manage as we always do.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Dia dhuit", "pronunciation": "jee-ah gwit", "meaning": "Hello (God to you)"}, {"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh khara", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Mhuise, the good Father has his books and his sermons, but I have my hands and the herbs that grew long before he came to this parish. 'Tis a foolish thing to scorn the yarrow for a bleeding or the mint for a troubled stomach just because they are old remedies. Let him speak of souls from the altar, sure, but when a woman is in the throes of labour, she'll be glad of my tea regardless of what was said on Sunday.
---
